/-!
# QOU.Machinery.HeckeH4WedderburnCoord — the 24×24 coordinate matrix of the rank-4 Wedderburn map

Wedderburn tranche **W3 (part 1 of 3)** (bean `qou-308a`; design provenance:
the H₄ Wedderburn design doc §4, session scratchpad
`h4_wedderburn_iso/design.md`): the matrix-literal layer of the inverse side
of $H_4(q) \cong K \times K \times M_2(K) \times M_3(K) \times M_3(K)$.

* `coordM` — the 24×24 coordinate matrix $A$ of the assembled map
  $(\chi_+, \chi_-, \rho_{22}, \rho_{31}, \rho_{211})$ on the subscript NF
  basis (rows `chi_plus, chi_minus, rho22_{00..11}, rho31_{00..22},
  rho211_{00..22}`, columns the 24 basis words), authored per-row
  (`coordMRow0 … coordMRow23`) in atom-separated form — only `q⁻¹`,
  `(q ^ 2 + 1)⁻¹`, `(q ^ 4 + q ^ 2 + 1)⁻¹`, `(q ^ 4 + 1)⁻¹` powers;
* `coordMInv` — its symbolic inverse $A^{-1}$ (sympy, 24×24 inversion over
  $\mathbb{Q}(q)$, from
  `computations/h4_wedderburn/hecke-h4-wedderburn-seminormal.data.json`).
  **Nothing is trusted from the computation** — the product identities are
  proved entry-by-entry in `HeckeH4WedderburnMul*.lean`;
* `wvec` / `homVec` — the stacked component vector and the stacked
  coordinates of the assembled map;
* `wedderburnInvV₄` — the inverse coordinate map, *defined* as the matrix
  action `(coordMInv q).mulVec (wvec …)`;
* `homVec_eq_mulVec` — the glue identifying the assembled map's stacked
  coordinates with `(coordM q).mulVec v` (24 coordinate goals, each closed
  by `ring` after unfolding — both sides are the same $A$-row applied to `v`).

HONESTY (STRICT, §"Axiomatization is NOT a Proof"): every lemma is
discharged by computation — no axiom, no `sorry`, no conditional class, no
`native_decide`; the sympy cofactors are *payloads*, re-verified by Lean's
`linear_combination` kernel check.

Base ring (§7c): generic `[Field K]` — inverses are genuinely used; no
archimedean construct.

-- Ref: [wenzl1988] https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01239526
-- Ref: [jones1987] https://doi.org/10.2307/1971403
-/

namespace QOU.Machinery.H4

set_option maxRecDepth 8000
